14:{what:"Finds blank cells in the employee data and fills them with the column average.",why:"Machine learning models cannot learn from blank cells.",pic:"Filling blank boxes in a marksheet with the class average.",where:"Employee, medical and survey data, which often have gaps.",libs:"pandas for the table. numpy is imported but not used directly.",words:[["NaN","Not a Number. pandas uses it for a missing value."],["Imputation","Filling missing values with a sensible guess."],["Mean","The average of the values that are present."],["loc","Selects rows and columns by label."]]},
15:{what:"Shrinks very large numbers with a logarithm so lopsided data becomes balanced, then compares boxplots.",why:"A few huge values (outliers) can confuse a model. Log makes them behave.",pic:"Zooming out so giant numbers fit on the page.",where:"Salaries, house prices, website traffic.",libs:"pandas, numpy, matplotlib (plots) and seaborn (imported but unused here).",words:[["Skew","Most values are small, with a long tail of big ones."],["Outlier","A value far away from the rest."],["Logarithm","Counts how many times you multiply by 10 to reach a number."],["Boxplot","A picture showing the middle of the data and its outliers."]]},
17:{what:"Reads a house dataset and splits it into training data and test data.",why:"You must test a model on data it has never seen, or the score is misleading.",pic:"Keeping a few questions secret for the final test.",where:"The first step of almost every machine learning project.",libs:"pandas to read the file, scikit-learn for train_test_split.",words:[["Feature (X)","The input columns the model learns from."],["Target (y)","The answer column we want to predict."],["Training set","The data used to teach the model."],["Test set","The data kept back to check the model."]]},
18:{what:"Fits a best-fit straight line from house size to price, then predicts the price of a 2000 sq ft house.",why:"It lets us predict a number from other numbers.",pic:"Drawing the best straight line through dots.",where:"House prices, sales forecasting, any number prediction.",libs:"scikit-learn for LinearRegression, numpy for the arrays.",words:[["Weight (w)","The slope: how much price rises per extra sq ft."],["Intercept (b)","Where the line starts."],["fit","Teach the model using the data."],["predict","Ask the model for an answer on new data."]]},
19:{what:"Measures how wrong a model's predictions are using MSE, RMSE and R squared.",why:"A score tells you whether your model is good or needs work.",pic:"A report card for your model's guesses.",where:"Checking any regression model.",libs:"numpy for maths, scikit-learn for the metric functions.",words:[["Error","Actual value minus predicted value."],["MSE","The average of the squared errors."],["RMSE","The square root of MSE, in the original units."],["R squared","How much of the pattern the model explains. 1 is perfect."]]},
